    "q-225139": {
      "dom_id": "step-q225139",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14121463/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2{v}^{2}+u,\\,y=u-{v}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed.",
      "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2{v}^{2}+u,\\,u-{v}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+u,\\,u-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2+u,\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2+u,\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2+t,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14121463/assets/2ac0e0eac9e4633a8d29881782d8dfe93c4c594ca0f99912a98507822c97c4b4.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
